      "text": "To find the greatest possible three-digit number divisible by 3 and by 6, we first need to understand that any number divisible by 6 must also be divisible by 3, since 6 is a factor of 3. Hence, we are looking for the greatest three-digit number that is divisible by 6.\n\n1. Identify the greatest three-digit number:\n  . The greatest three-digit number is 999.\n\n2. Determine the divisibility condition:\n  . A number is divisible by 6 if and only if it is divisible by both 2 and 3.\n\n3. Check divisibility by 2:\n  . For a number to be divisible by 2, its last digit must be even.\n\n4. Find the greatest even number less than or equal to 999:\n  . The largest even number less than or equal to 999 is 998.\n\n5. Verify divisibility by 3:\n  . A number is divisible by 3 if the sum of its digits is divisible by 3.\n\n6. Calculate the sum of the digits of 998:\n  . The sum of the digits of 998 is 9 + 9 + 8 = 26.\n\nSince 26 is not divisible by 3, we cannot have 998 as our answer.\n\n7. Adjust the number to the greatest three-digit number divisible by 6:\n  . Since 996 is divisible by 3 (9 + 9 + 6 = 24, which is divisible by 3) but not by 2 (it ends in 6, but we need it to end in 0 to be divisible by 2), the next number after 996 that meets both conditions is 990.\n   \n990 ends in 0, which is divisible by 2, and its digits sum up to 9 + 9 + 0 = 18, which is divisible by 3. Hence, 990 becomes the answer that satisfies both conditions.\n\n990 is the greatest three-digit number that is divisible by 3 and by 6. The answer is \\boxed{990} The greatest possible three-digit number is 990.",
